# LEGACY override reference. Copy to .env and fill in real values; .env is gitignored.
# The deployment surface is tezcatlipoca.toml (copy tezcatlipoca.example.toml), read by
# estate/config.py; anything set here reaches the config as an env override, and the
# environment beats the profile file. create-competition.py/deploy.py still load this file.
#
# NAMING STANDARD — every variable read by this codebase is one of exactly four kinds:
#   TEZ_<NAME>       a config override named after its key: TEZ_ + the key upper-cased with
#                    dots as underscores (proxmox.endpoint -> TEZ_PROXMOX_ENDPOINT); knobs.*
#                    drop the prefix (knobs.thin_headroom -> TEZ_THIN_HEADROOM).
#   TF_VAR_<name>    a legacy name estate/config.py still accepts (the terraform-era
#                    spelling), plus two env-only variables the code reads directly:
#                    TF_VAR_scoring_vm_id and TF_VAR_team_identifiers.
#   NAKON_*/VULNDB_*  vendored-tool config, read by vendor/nakon, not by this repo.
#   PROXMOX_*        the TLS-pin namespace (legacy names for proxmox.ca_bundle /
#                    proxmox.tls_fingerprint).
# A variable that is none of these must be declared in tests/test_secret_hygiene.py's
# INTERNAL_ENV allowlist with a reason. tests/test_secret_hygiene.py fails the suite if
# code reads an env var that is neither documented here nor allowlisted — that is what
# keeps this list from drifting again.
# Per-variable detail: docs/usage-people.md "Configure the event".
# 2026-08-06 and 2026-10-01 git-history secret-leak audits: docs/known-issues.md.

# --- Proxmox connection (profile: [proxmox]) ---
TEZ_PROXMOX_ENDPOINT=https://PROXMOX-HOST:8006
TEZ_PROXMOX_API_TOKEN=CHANGEME
TEZ_PROXMOX_NODE=CHANGEME
TEZ_PROXMOX_DATASTORE=CHANGEME
# Legacy spellings the config still accepts (use instead of the names above):
#TF_VAR_proxmox_endpoint=
#TF_VAR_proxmox_api_token=
#TF_VAR_proxmox_node=
#TF_VAR_datastore=
# Optional Proxmox API TLS verification (default: unverified self-signed cert). Set ONE to pin:
#PROXMOX_CA_BUNDLE=/etc/pve/pve-root-ca.pem
#PROXMOX_TLS_FINGERPRINT=AA:BB:CC:DD:...   # sha256 of the pveproxy cert

# --- SSH keypair (profile: [ssh]; agent, nakon) ---
TEZ_SSH_PUBLIC_KEY=ssh-ed25519 CHANGEME range-automation
TEZ_SSH_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=../proxmox
# Legacy: TF_VAR_ssh_public_key / TF_VAR_ssh_private_key_path.

# --- Scoring engine template (profile: [proxmox] / [engine]) ---
TEZ_PROXMOX_TEMPLATE_VM_ID=CHANGEME
TEZ_ENGINE_USERNAME=sysadmin            # legacy: TF_VAR_vm_username
# Static mgmt IP for the engine. Leave unset to use DEFAULT_ENGINE_MGMT_IP.
#TEZ_ENGINE_MGMT_IP=10.0.0.250
# Gateway for that static address. Leave unset to use DEFAULT_ENGINE_MGMT_GW.
#TEZ_ENGINE_MGMT_GW=10.0.0.1
# Override the engine VMID (default 1000) when 1000 is taken on the node. Env-only (not a
# profile key); --scoring-vmid wins over it.
#TF_VAR_scoring_vm_id=1000

# --- Per-competition inputs — NOT config ---
# The team roster with its passwords, the box list, the box login and the event name are read
# or generated from the competition dir (collect_teams(), users.json, the box picker) and land
# in the state document, teams.json and credentials.txt. A stale value here cannot override them.
# Pin team subnets explicitly instead of deriving them from the team index. Env-only.
# Comma/space separated identifiers, e.g. "221,222,223".
#TF_VAR_team_identifiers=101,102

# --- tezcatlipoca tuning knobs (profile: [knobs]) ---
# Count this fraction (0 < x <= 1) of the *provisioned* disk math toward the datastore
# headroom preflight, for thin-provisioned pools where provisioned >> used.
# Omitted, the check uses full provisioned size. See docs/known-issues.md.
#TEZ_THIN_HEADROOM=0.5
# Blue agent cycle budget in seconds (default 600, calibrated on the GX10 qwen-flash).
# Raise it for a slower LLM endpoint: run-e9d17d65's qwen3.8-flash-next needed ~3 min
# per 10-token reply and every 600 s cycle timed out, so blue never engaged.
#TEZ_CYCLE_TIMEOUT=1500
# Interpreter for the harness's OPERATOR-SIDE children (the deploy, verify-competition,
# scrim-report and `-m badauto` spawns). Default: the interpreter the harness itself runs
# under (sys.executable) — which is what a host whose engine deps live in a venv needs
# (2026-10-14: two dashboard runs died on a bare python3 without toml). Set this only when
# those dependencies live in a different environment on the run host.
#TEZ_PYTHON=
# Concurrent ranges are supported (2026-10-04): the preflight WARNS when another deploy
# is in flight on this host and proceeds. Give each range its own --scoring-vmid and
# TF_VAR_team_identifiers blocks — the preflight refuses real collisions, and run-id
# ownership tags scope every teardown to its own deploy's VMs.
# Red's routed segment (bad-auto's deploy.red_subnet) when red01 runs in routed mode.
# Set it and every satellite jump VM accepts red -> local team subnets and SNATs red to
# the team gateway (192.168.<id>.1), exactly like engine -> box traffic — without it red's
# source matches no FORWARD rule and is dropped (scale8 soak 2026-10-02: 15/15 cred_sprays
# failed "unreachable over SSH" against satellite teams, 0 footholds). Unset/empty leaves
# the rules out, which is right for single-node ranges and masq-mode red. Read the value
# from bad-auto's config.yaml `deploy.red_subnet` (default 10.200.0.0/24).
# See docs/multi-node.md "Routed red".
#TEZ_RED_SEGMENT=10.200.0.0/24
# run-agent-scrim.py — red01's Proxmox template NAME, when the node's differs from
# bad-auto's built-in default. Per-host template names: infrastructure-status.md.
# Same as passing --red-template on every scrim.
#TEZ_RED_TEMPLATE=

# --- Multi-node (optional) ---
# nodes.json references per-host API tokens BY ENV-VAR NAME; give each additional host's
# token its own variable here (e.g. TEZ_PROXMOX_API_TOKEN_SITE2=...). The name is whatever
# that record's token_env says. See docs/multi-node.md. Absent nodes.json, everything is
# single-node.

# Competition definitions live outside the engine (tezcatlipoca-competitions); the drivers
# resolve --competition NAME under knobs.comps_root when set (else ./competitions, else the
# sibling ../tezcatlipoca-competitions checkout). Canonical TEZ_COMPS_ROOT; legacy
# TEZ_COMPETITIONS_ROOT.
#TEZ_COMPS_ROOT=

# test-artifacts.py — where test-run folders are archived outside the repo (the
# default ~/.tezcatlipoca/automated-tests survives discarding a practice worktree).
#TEZ_ARTIFACTS_ARCHIVE=

# vmid for the pfsense-provision template the builder tool creates (tools/build-pfsense-provision-template.py)
#TEZ_PFSENSE_PROVISION_VMID=957

# vyos-provision template builder knobs (tools/build-vyos-provision-template.py)
#TEZ_VYOS_VMID=959
#TEZ_VYOS_ISO_URL=https://community-downloads.vyos.dev/stream/2026.03/vyos-2026.03-generic-amd64.iso
#TEZ_VYOS_ISO_STORAGE=local
#TEZ_VYOS_STORAGE=hdd
#TEZ_VYOS_BRIDGE=vmbr0
#TEZ_VYOS_PW=
# qemu-guest-agent retrofit knobs (tools/build-pfsense-provision-template.py agent-*):
# scratch VM vmid, node bridge for the SSH hop, package branch override (default: the
# guest's own ABI), and the dev-host staging dir for the downloaded .pkg closure.
#TEZ_PFSENSE_QGA_VMID=958
#TEZ_QGA_BRIDGE=vmbr0
#TEZ_QGA_PKG_BRANCH=
#TEZ_QGA_STAGE_DIR=/tmp/pfsense-qga-pkgs

# --- Remote access (headscale) — profile: [headscale] ---
# Every deploy enrolls the scoring engine as a headscale subnet router and scopes
# participant access to their own team's network (docs/remote-access.md). The
# pipeline administers headscale over ssh+sudo on its host; these four are REQUIRED
# unless the Compfile opts out with `remote_access 0`.
TEZ_HEADSCALE_URL=https://headscale.example
TEZ_HEADSCALE_SSH_HOST=203.0.113.10
TEZ_HEADSCALE_SSH_USER=sysadmin
TEZ_HEADSCALE_SSH_PASSWORD=
# Owner of the router nodes (must exist on the headscale; default cyberrange-infra).
#TEZ_HEADSCALE_INFRA_USER=cyberrange-infra
# Participant preauth-key lifetime (headscale duration syntax; default 72h).
# Canonical TEZ_HEADSCALE_KEY_TTL; legacy TEZ_REMOTE_KEY_TTL.
#TEZ_HEADSCALE_KEY_TTL=72h

# Student portal (Compfile `portal 1`; docs/portal.md; profile [portal]). Put the tunnel pair
# ONLY in the event's env file — a practice worktree that carries it would claim the stable
# public hostname.
#   TEZ_PORTAL_TUNNEL_TOKEN  Cloudflare tunnel token (unset = portal reachable via ssh -L only)
#   TEZ_PORTAL_HOSTNAME      that tunnel's public hostname, for the one-owner check
#   TEZ_PORTAL_OPEN=1        start with team console access OPEN (practice runs; default closed)
#TEZ_PORTAL_TUNNEL_TOKEN=
#TEZ_PORTAL_HOSTNAME=range.example.com
#TEZ_PORTAL_OPEN=1
#   Reset-request sync (portal → run-queue dashboard): the DASHBOARD sets both in the
#   deploy run's env; portal_ops writes them into the portal's engine-side .env and the
#   portal pushes team reset requests to the dashboard's bell with them. Never set by
#   hand here.
#TEZ_OPS_URL=
#TEZ_OPS_TOKEN=
